Optimize the EcoPredict landing page scroll performance.

IMPORTANT:
Do NOT redesign the landing page.
Do NOT remove the cinematic scroll-driven Earth animation.
Keep the existing visual design, sections, typography, content, and interactions.

The current scroll experience is lagging/stuttering, especially while the Earth frame animation changes.

Optimize the implementation for smooth 60 FPS scrolling.

==================================================
1. OPTIMIZE SCROLL EVENT HANDLING
==================================================

Do NOT perform React state updates on every scroll event.

Do NOT use expensive calculations directly inside:

window.addEventListener("scroll", ...)

Use requestAnimationFrame to throttle scroll-driven updates.

Use a pattern similar to:

let ticking = false;

window.addEventListener("scroll", () => {
    if (!ticking) {
        requestAnimationFrame(() => {
            updateScrollProgress();
            ticking = false;
        });

        ticking = true;
    }
});

The animation loop should run at most once per browser frame.

Prefer passive listeners:

window.addEventListener("scroll", handler, { passive: true });

==================================================
2. DO NOT RE-RENDER THE ENTIRE REACT COMPONENT
==================================================

The Earth frame animation must NOT cause the entire landing page to re-render.

Avoid:

setCurrentFrame(...)

on every scroll update if that causes a full React component render.

Use:

- useRef
- requestAnimationFrame
- direct canvas drawing

where appropriate.

The scroll position should control the animation without triggering unnecessary React renders.

==================================================
3. USE HTML CANVAS FOR THE EARTH FRAMES
==================================================

If the current implementation uses <img> elements for the 180 Earth frames, replace the frame-rendering mechanism with a single HTML canvas.

Do NOT render 180 images simultaneously in the DOM.

Use:

<canvas>

and draw only the currently required frame:

ctx.drawImage(images[frameIndex], ...)

This should dramatically reduce DOM/layout overhead.

==================================================
4. PRELOAD EARTH FRAMES INTELLIGENTLY
==================================================

There are approximately 180 WebP Earth frames.

Do NOT load all 180 high-resolution images simultaneously if that causes memory pressure.

Implement progressive loading.

Priority:

1. Load the first few frames immediately.
2. Load nearby frames around the current scroll position.
3. Continue loading remaining frames in the background.
4. Keep already-loaded frames cached.
5. Never block scrolling while frames are loading.

Use:

HTMLImageElement.decode()

where supported.

Example concept:

const img = new Image();
img.src = frameUrl;
await img.decode();

Do not decode dozens of images simultaneously.

Use a small concurrency limit.

==================================================
5. PRELOAD BASED ON SCROLL POSITION
==================================================

When the user is near frame 40:

Prioritize:

35–45

Then gradually preload:

30–50

Do not prioritize frames hundreds of positions away from the current frame.

Use a rolling preload window.

==================================================
6. USE DEVICE PIXEL RATIO CAREFULLY
==================================================

Do not create an unnecessarily huge canvas.

Use a reasonable devicePixelRatio limit.

For example:

const dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, 2);

This prevents extremely high-resolution rendering from consuming excessive GPU/CPU resources.

==================================================
7. AVOID CANVAS RESIZING DURING SCROLL
==================================================

Do not repeatedly change:

canvas.width
canvas.height

during scrolling.

Calculate the canvas dimensions during:

- initial load
- window resize

Then keep those dimensions stable while scrolling.

==================================================
8. OPTIMIZE IMAGE DRAWING
==================================================

Draw the Earth using:

ctx.clearRect(...)
ctx.drawImage(...)

Avoid creating new canvas elements or Image objects for every frame.

Reuse the same canvas and image objects.

Do not convert images to base64 on every frame.

Do not create blobs repeatedly.

==================================================
9. SEPARATE SCROLL PROGRESS FROM PAGE ANIMATION
==================================================

Calculate normalized scroll progress:

progress = scrollPosition / scrollableSectionHeight

Then convert:

progress → frame index

For example:

frameIndex = Math.floor(progress * (TOTAL_FRAMES - 1))

Do not use multiple independent scroll listeners for the Earth.

Create one centralized scroll animation controller.

==================================================
10. REDUCE OTHER SCROLL-BASED EFFECTS
==================================================

Audit the page for:

- parallax
- blur
- backdrop-filter
- box-shadow animation
- filters
- transforms
- opacity changes
- Framer Motion scroll listeners

Do not remove the visual effects completely.

Instead:

- prefer transform and opacity
- avoid layout-triggering properties
- reduce expensive blur
- avoid animating width/height/top/left
- avoid unnecessary backdrop-filter
- use GPU-friendly transforms

Prefer:

transform: translate3d(...)
opacity

instead of:

top
left
width
height

==================================================
11. FRAMER MOTION OPTIMIZATION
==================================================

The project currently uses Framer Motion.

Audit all scroll animations.

Do not create dozens of useScroll/useTransform subscriptions unnecessarily.

Use a small number of shared motion values where possible.

Avoid animating large DOM trees on every scroll frame.

Use:

will-change: transform;

ONLY on elements that are actually animated.

Do not apply will-change to the entire page.

==================================================
12. EARTH CANVAS POSITIONING
==================================================

Keep the Earth visually fixed/sticky while the story content scrolls.

Use:

position: sticky;

where appropriate rather than continuously calculating the Earth position with JavaScript.

The scroll should control the Earth frame, not its physical DOM position.

==================================================
13. MOBILE OPTIMIZATION
==================================================

On mobile devices:

- reduce Earth canvas resolution slightly
- limit devicePixelRatio to 1.5–2
- reduce preload concurrency
- avoid expensive blur effects
- respect prefers-reduced-motion

Do not break the cinematic experience.

==================================================
14. REDUCED MOTION
==================================================

Respect:

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)

When enabled:

- reduce or disable frame-by-frame animation
- keep the page usable
- do not create rapid motion

==================================================
15. CLEAN UP EVENT LISTENERS
==================================================

When the landing component unmounts:

- remove scroll listeners
- cancel requestAnimationFrame
- cancel pending animation work
- release unnecessary image references

Avoid memory leaks.

==================================================
16. PERFORMANCE TARGET
==================================================

Target:

60 FPS scrolling on modern desktop hardware.

Scrolling should feel continuous even while the Earth frame changes.

There should be:

NO:
- visible stutter
- frame flashing
- delayed scroll response
- excessive CPU usage
- excessive memory usage
- repeated image downloads
- React re-rendering of the entire page per frame

==================================================
17. IMPORTANT: DO NOT BREAK THE EARTH EXPERIENCE
==================================================

The final result must still have:

- cinematic Earth
- scroll-driven Earth rotation/animation
- existing story sections
- existing EcoPredict branding
- existing navigation
- existing CTA buttons
- existing visual hierarchy

Only the underlying performance implementation should change.

==================================================
18. VERIFY USING BROWSER DEVTOOLS
==================================================

After optimization:

1. Open Chrome DevTools.
2. Open Performance.
3. Record scrolling through the entire landing page.
4. Check FPS.
5. Check long tasks.
6. Check excessive scripting.
7. Check memory usage.
8. Check network requests.
9. Confirm Earth frames are not downloaded repeatedly.
10. Confirm there are no console errors.

Fix any remaining performance bottlenecks.

FINAL GOAL:

Make the existing EcoPredict cinematic landing page feel smooth, responsive, and premium while preserving the current design and Earth animation.
